A||||14||Andy||oil change
Z||000000||Andy||06-17-99||07:23 PM||||I need to change the oil and filter. Any suggestions what is the best grade of oil to use.I'm planning to use syncetic oil.Also whats the best filter.
Z||000001||theogre||06-17-99||08:02 PM||||Fiero uses 5W-30 in the 4 cyl. I think the V6 is the same. In summer months 10W-30 is fine. (the W number is only relevant in cold temperatures. Chemicals in the oil actually thin it out when cold.) Many people swear by synthetic oils. I haven't used them yet... When you drive 30K miles a year you change allot of oil, and synthetic so far has been outside my price range. I'm going to try the "improved" Quaker State this next change. The stuff they claim protects past 3000 miles. It gets realy hard sometimes to change the oil every other month.<p>For filters stay with one of the main branded lines. I generally use Fram, Purolator, AC/Delco(on GM), or MotorCraft(on ford). I've found that the shells on many store brand filters, and filters used by "quick lube" places are often soda can thin. If they are scrimping this way on the shell.... they are doing it internally as well.<p>Because GM and Ford commonly keep their number of filter types down, older cars benifit from newer filter specifications. For example,,, Ford has used the 1A filter can on nearly all of it's cars. The internals of that filter however have evolved to meet todays tighter standards. The same can fits a 99 5l motor fits a 68 289cid motor. The 289 gets the benifit of the improved spec required by the 5l.<p>My point is that any major branded filter you get is likely better than anything available at the time Fiero was built. Is there a best? it depends on who's test data you read. There are a number of testing methods. If you check some of the brands web sites, you'll quickly see what I mean.
Z||000002||ezsurf||06-17-99||08:40 PM||||Andy,<br>The 2.8 engine uses 5W-30 as well. The W stands for winter. Synthetic oils will flow better at colder temperatures, and probably have a higher bake point as well. Synthetic oils are a LITTLE better than conventional oil.  However, the primary reason for changing your oil is to remove acids (mainly sulfuric) and other contaminants in the oil. Gasoline has a small amount of sulfer in it. Sulfuric acid is produced as a by-product of combustion, sulfuric acid mixes with your oil and builds up in the crankcase.  The only way to get the acid out is to periodically change the oil. This applies to snythetic oil as well.  There used to be a large difference between engine oils.  Any NAME BRAND oil today has sufficient additives to give excellent performance (reference consumer reports tests). So, if you change your oil with synthetic every 3k-5k miles it's going to get expensive, but it's still excellent oil. As far as filters go, I wouldn't use anything but a Wix filter. You will probably find that most people who race cars use the same. 
Z||000003||Bruce||06-17-99||09:32 PM||||What about the K&N oil filter? Is it that much better, or is it just hype?
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||06-17-99||09:37 PM||||I am a believer in synthetic oils ever since I left 2 bottles out on the porch in the middle of winter. I wanted to see if the synthetic oil would flow faster than conventional oil. Pennzoil 5w30 and Mobil 1 are the 2 I left outside at around 10 degrees farenheit. We have been having some very strong winds too,I think with the wind chill it was about -5. I woke the next morning to see what the result would be. Both containers were unopened and brand new. The Pennzoil moved pretty slow as I tipped it upside down. Then I picked up the Mobil 1 and it seemed like it was at room temperature. Ever since then I use Mobile 1 in the block and tranny as well as synthetic brake fluid in both the clutch and brake master cylinders. I think it has a higher tolerance of heat too. <p>OIL IS CHEAPER THAN STEEL!   Just remember that no matter what oil you use and change it often.
Z||000005||SCCA FIERO||06-17-99||10:12 PM||||Oh, I forgot to say the weight of the Moble 1 oill. It was 5w30 as well.<p>Bruce, I have never used a K&N oil filter,but I hear it has a valve that keeps the oil from going into the pan after the motor is shut off. This is supposed to keep the oil up inside the motor so when you start it again the internal parts have an oil supply to stop dry-starts. Dry starts are supposed to account for 80% of engine wear. This could be a good thing I guess,but what if that valve gets jammed or stuck shut?
Z||000006||ezsurf||06-18-99||06:41 AM||||There is one other thing about synthetic oil.  If you just rebuilt your engine, I would wait 10k to 20k miles before switching to synthetic.  Synthetic oil is slicker and CAN prevent the rings from seating.  This will cause the engine to burn oil throughout it's lifespan until the engine is rebuilt again.
Z||000007||grizco||06-18-99||05:16 PM||||I use regular 5W30 motor oil up here in Alaska. I've never had a problem with insufficient oil pressure until it gets down to -65 or colder (without the windchill). Of course I have block heaters and oil pan heaters on my vehicles and plug them in for a minimal of four hours when its colder than around 15 above. 
Z||000008||batboy||06-18-99||07:51 PM||||I don't mean to be nitpicking, but the subject of windchill has surface twice in this discussion. Windchill has little effect on inanimate objects such as oil and cars. Windchill is the measure of how cold it feels on exposed flesh.
Z||000009||ezsurf||06-18-99||08:52 PM||||Good point Batman!
Z||000010||Andy||06-19-99||06:42 PM||||Thank's for all the reply's,went and got some 5W-30 Oil and a Fram filter today,I'm planning to change it tomorrow, if it don't rain.The oil is pretty dirty and I don't know how long it has been, since the Oil was changed.Just bought the car not to long ago.<br>What about some 10W-40 for the next Oilchange,was told by a backyard mechanic to use it,since the engine[V 6] has 86000 miles on it and therefore needs a thicker Oil,also living in the southeast we don't have to worry about 0 degree weather.I'm not planning on using it, but would like to know what you all think about it.
Z||000011||batboy||06-19-99||08:25 PM||||Modern cars use 10W30 (5W30 in cold weather) because the bearing tolerances are closer (or tighter) than in the "olden" days. Worn out engines that burn a lot of oil might benefit somewhat from using a slightly thicker oil such as 10W40 in order to give the bearings a little more "cushion" so to speak. Nowadays, 86,000 miles is relatively low mileage. You didn't say if it was burning much oil. I wouldn't use the heavier oil except in the case of excessive oil consumation (burning it not leaking it). Even then, it's merely a matter of time before you have to yank the motor and rebuild it.
Z||000012||PaUL||06-20-99||07:25 PM||||Hey, Ive got one more oil change tip. If you look up the 2.8 v6 fiero in the application book for oil filters, it will specify PF47 for the AC brand filter. Ive found that the PF52 filter has the same threads and is used on 2.8s in other cars, but is twice as big as the PF47. It holds more oil and must be better than a smaller filter. Its easier to install and remove too since it sticks out more. Other brands must make an equivalent size to AC PF52. 
Z||000013||theogre||06-21-99||12:30 AM||||that option is also valid for the 4cyl.
Z||000014||PaUL||06-21-99||03:22 AM||||hehe you finally agree with me on something ogre.
